  setLiteral(11372, "How far away is the voice?");
  setLiteral(11373, "Why does a small room sound boxy?");
  setLiteral(11374, "Hard walls, soft walls");
  setLiteral(11375, "What makes a room sound big?");
  setLiteral(11376, "A voice behind a door");
  setLiteral(11377, "Turn the speaker away");
  setLiteral(11378, "Three parts of every sound");
  setLiteral(11379, "A phone call in a car");
  setLiteral(11380, "Why does a passing car change its pitch?");
  setLiteral(11381, "Sound bends around a wall");
  setLiteral(11382, "The sound that travels straight from the source to your ears \342\200\223 the first and loudest part. It tells you where the source is, and it gets 6 dB quieter with every doubling of the distance.");
  setLiteral(11383, "The first echoes from walls, floor and ceiling, within about 50\342\200\22380 ms. You do not hear them as echoes: they melt into the direct sound and tell your brain the size and shape of the room.");
  setLiteral(11384, "Thousands of reflections that come from everywhere and fade away together. It has no direction any more \342\200\223 it tells how big the room is and what its surfaces are made of.");
  setLiteral(11385, "Reverb time: how long the sound needs to fade away by 60 dB, to a thousandth. A car about 0.1 s, a living room 0.5 s, a classroom 0.7 s, a church 5 s.");
  setLiteral(11386, "How much of the sound a surface swallows instead of reflecting it: 0 = a perfect mirror (tiles and glass about 0.02), 1 = nothing comes back (an open window). A heavy curtain 0.45, mineral wool 0.85.");
  setLiteral(11387, "The distance from the source where direct sound and reverb are equally loud \342\200\223 the dashed circle. Closer: clear and present. Further: the room takes over.");
  setLiteral(11388, "In small rooms some bass notes fit exactly between two walls and ring on longer than the rest \342\200\223 the room sounds boomy or boxy. In a corner they are at their loudest.");
  setLiteral(11389, "How much quieter the sound gets through a wall, door or window: 25 dB a light interior door, 45 dB a brick wall. Bass gets through more easily than treble \342\200\223 that is why music next door sounds muffled.");
  setLiteral(11390, "Close up, the direct sound is much louder than the room: dry and present. Far away it drops 6 dB per doubling of the distance while the reverb stays almost the same \342\200\223 the voice sinks into the room. On the dashed circle both are equally loud.");
  setLiteral(11391, "In a small hard room a few bass notes fit exactly between the walls and ring on \342\200\223 room modes. In a corner they are all at their loudest. Big rooms have so many modes that they melt into a smooth reverb.");
  setLiteral(11392, "Soft surfaces swallow part of the sound at every bounce, so the reverb dies faster and gets darker \342\200\223 the room sounds dry like a vocal booth. Hard tiles and glass reflect almost everything and keep it ringing.");
  setLiteral(11393, "The time between the direct sound and the first reflections tells your brain how far away the walls are. A big room has a long gap and a long reverb \342\200\223 together they sound big. A long reverb alone only sounds like a hard room.");
  setLiteral(11394, "A closed door lets only part of the sound through, mostly the bass \342\200\223 the voice gets quiet and muffled. Open, the sound comes through the opening and bends around its edges: louder, clearer, and it seems to come from the door.");
  setLiteral(11395, "Voices and loudspeakers send the treble mainly forward, the bass in all directions. Turned away, you hear less direct treble and more room \342\200\223 darker and further away, even at the same distance.");
  setLiteral(11396, "The direct sound tells where the source is, the early reflections the size and shape of the room, the reverb how big and how hard it is. In a film mix these three parts are often shaped separately.");
  setLiteral(11397, "The tiny, soft car gives almost no reverb \342\200\223 only very early reflections that color the voice. The telephone cuts the bass below 300 Hz and the treble above 3.4 kHz. Both together: a call from a car.");
  setLiteral(11398, "Three things happen at once. Doppler: while the source comes closer, each wave arrives a little earlier than the last \342\200\223 the pitch is higher; moving away, lower. At 5 m/s that is only 1.5 %, at 30 m/s (a car) 9 % \342\200\223 a whole tone. Direction: the ear nearer to the source hears it up to 0.7 ms earlier and louder, the head shadows the treble on the far side \342\200\223 this is what moves the sound across the stereo field. Distance: level and treble rise as it comes close and fall again.");
  setLiteral(11399, "A wall stops the straight path, but sound bends around edges \342\200\223 the lower the frequency (the longer the wave), the better. Over a low wall the bass arrives almost as if there were no wall, the treble is shadowed. Through the wall itself only the bass gets through: everything behind a wall sounds muffled, and a lowered wall gives the treble back first.");
